capability text/split-shell  the words a POSIX shell reads in a line, by its quoting
  heroes  strings.split_shell
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  shlex.split, shlex.shlex
  note    `/bin/sh`'s reading, which Rust's crate shlex 1.3.0 (`shlex::split`) and `/bin/sh` answered alike on 2026-11-17: a backslash before a line feed taken away, a backslash inside double quotes taking `$`, a backquote, `"`, a backslash and a line feed as themselves, a comment from a `#` that begins a word, a carriage return part of a word. Python's `shlex.split` keeps the backslash before `$` and a backquote, keeps the line feed after a backslash, reads no comment unless asked (`comments=True`) and splits at a carriage return; its `shlex.shlex`, a lexer with its own sets of characters, is read here in its POSIX form with `whitespace_split`. An unclosed quote and a backslash that ends the line fail `invalid`, where Python raises `ValueError` and the crate answers `None`. Nothing is expanded and no operator is read, as neither of them does

