capability text/split-windows-command-line  the arguments of a Windows command line, the program name first
  heroes  strings.split_windows_args
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the C runtime's rules since Visual Studio 2008 ("Parsing C++ command-line arguments"), the program name by its own rule: two double quotes inside a quoted part are one quote and the part goes on; the empty line has no arguments, where `CommandLineToArgvW` answers the running program's path. Run on Windows on 2026-11-18 against the UCRT's own `argv` (a child started by `CreateProcessW` with the line as it stands) and shell32's `CommandLineToArgvW`, three lines: the function agreed with the C runtime on all three, and `CommandLineToArgvW` read `p "a""b" c` as two arguments, `p` and `a"b c`, making one quote of the two and closing the part. Decided 2026-11-18 (repairs-3): the C runtime is the rule followed, since it is what a program's `argv` holds and what `quote_windows_args` writes for (Python's `list2cmdline` writes for it too); the doc said both until then and now says where shell32 parts, and that line is a test. Go's `golang.org/x/sys/windows.DecomposeCommandLine` and Rust's `std::env::args` on Windows are not on this Mac and were not asked

